sec.p:
Expanding upon Pu and Tomas’ methodology to represent out system, we added one dimension to track the calcium concentration, six dimensions for each possible state of the LVA channel, and two dimensions for each possible state of the BK channel. The state vectors for each ion channel are therefore defined as follows: a five-component vector NK for the K+ gates, an eight-component vector NNa for the Na+ gates, a six-component vector NLVA for the LVA gates, and a two-component vector NBK for the BK gates. The state vectors are represented as:
(1)
NK=[nK0,nK1,nK2,nK3,nK4]T∈[0,1]5 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} N^{K}=\left[n_{K 0}, n_{K 1}, n_{K 2}, n_{K 3}, n_{K 4}\right]^{T} \in[0,1]^{5} \tag{1} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(2)
NNa=[nNa00,nNa10,nNa20,nNa30,nNa01,nNa11,nNa21,nNa31]T∈[0,1]8 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} N^{N a}=\left[n_{N a 00}, n_{N a 10}, n_{N a 20}, n_{N a 30}, n_{N a 01}, n_{N a 11}, n_{N a 21}, n_{N a 31}\right]^{T} \in[0,1]^{8} \tag{2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(3)
NLVA=[nLVA00,nLVA10,nLVA20,nLVA01,nLVA11,nLVA21]T∈[0,1]6 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} N^{L V A}=\left[n_{L V A 00}, n_{L V A 10}, n_{L V A 20}, n_{L V A 01}, n_{L V A 11}, n_{L V A 21}\right]^{T} \in[0,1]^{6} \tag{3} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(4)
NBK=[nBK0,nBK1]T∈[0,1]2 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} N^{B K}=\left[n_{B K 0}, n_{B K 1}\right]^{T} \in[0,1]^{2} \tag{4} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The K+ channel consists of four independent n (activation) gates, forming a five-vertex channel-state diagram with eight directed edges. The channel conducts a current only when it is in the rightmost state (Figure 2D). Similarly, the Na+ channel is composed of three identical n gates and one h (inactivation) gate, forming an 8-vertex diagram with 20 directed edges, one of which is conducting. The LVA channel includes two identical n gates and one h gate, resulting in a 6-vertex diagram with 14 directed edges, one of which is conducting. The BK channel consists of a single n gate, forming a 2-vertex diagram with two directed edges, one of which is conducting. The Markov chain for every channel is shown in Figure 2B.

For a sufficiently large number of channels, Schmidt and Thomas (2014) and Schmidt et al. (2018) demonstrated that, under voltage clamp, the equations governing the system can be approximated by a multidimensional Ornstein-Uhlenbeck (OU) process, or Langevin equation, as follows:

(5)

dNion=∑k=1kmaxζion{αkion(V)Ni(k)iondt+ϵionαkionNi(k)iondWkion}
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} d N^{i o n}=\sum_{k=1}^{k_{\max }} \zeta^{i o n}\left\{\alpha_{k}^{i o n}(V) N_{i(k)}^{i o n} d t+\sqrt{\epsilon^{i o n} \alpha_{k}^{i o n} N_{i(k)}^{i o n} d W_{k}^{i o n}}\right\} \tag{5} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



Here ζionis the stoichiometry vector for the kth directed edge. If we write i(k) for the source node and j(k) for the destination node of edge k, then ζion=ej(k)ion−ei(k)ion\begin{document}$\zeta^{i o n}=e_{j(k)}^{i o n}-e_{i(k)}^{i o n}$\end{document} . The voltage-dependent per capita transition rate along the kth edge is αkion(V)\begin{document}$\alpha_{k}^{i o n}(V)$\end{document} and Ni(k)ion\begin{document}$N_{i(k)}^{i o n}$\end{document} is the fractional occupancy of the source node for the kth transition. The finite-time increment in the Poisson process is approximated by a gaussian process, namely, the increment dWkion\begin{document}$d W_{k}^{\text {ion }}$\end{document} in a Wiener (Brownian motion) process associated with each directed edge. These independent noise terms are scaled by ϵion=1NTotion\begin{document}$\epsilon^{i o n}=\frac{1}{N_{\text {Tot }}^{i o n}}$\end{document}, where 1NTotion\begin{document}$\frac{1}{N_{\text {Tot }}^{\text {ion }}}$\end{document} represents the total number of that specific ion channel.

The membrane potential is governed by the current balance equation:

(6)

CdVdt=∑χIχ
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} C \frac{d V}{d t}=\sum_{\chi} I_{\chi} \tag{6} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



where the set χ contains the six ionic currents included in the model:

(7)

χ∈{LVA,Na,K,BK,L,ORCO}
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \chi \in\{L V A, N a, K, B K, L, O R C O\}$$\end{document}



Each of the six ionic currents included in the model are described as:

(8)

INa=−gNaN8Na(V−VNa)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} I_{N a}=-g_{N a} N_{8}^{N a}\left(V-V_{N a}\right) \tag{8} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}




(9)

IK=−gKN5K(V−VK)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} I_{K}=-g_{K} N_{5}^{K}\left(V-V_{K}\right) \tag{9} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}




(10)

ILVA=−gLVAN6LVA(V−VLVA)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} I_{L V A}=-g_{L V A} N_{6}^{L V A}\left(V-V_{L V A}\right) \tag{10} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}




(11)

IBK=−gBKN2BK(V−VBK)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} I_{B K}=-g_{B K} N_{2}^{B K}\left(V-V_{B K}\right) \tag{11} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}




(12)

IOrco=−gOrco(0.8+0.2sin⁡(2πt24h))(V−VOrco)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} I_{\text {Orco }}=-g_{\text {Orco }}\left(0.8+0.2 \sin \left(\frac{2 \pi t}{24 h}\right)\right)\left(V-V_{\text {Orco }}\right) \tag{12} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}




(13)

IL=−gL(VL−V)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} I_{L}=-g_{L}\left(V_{L}-V\right) \tag{13} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



Here, gχ is the maximal conductance, V is the membrane potential, and Vχ is the reversal potential for each ion current. The parameter values for each current can be found in Table 1.

Each gate’s steady state activation function, except for the BK channel, takes the form:
(15)
pj∞ion(V)=1.01.0+exp⁡((V−θp)βp) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} p_{j \infty}^{i o n}(V)=\frac{1.0}{1.0+\exp \left(\frac{\left(V-\theta_{p}\right)}{\beta_{p}}\right)} \tag{15} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The IBK current is dependent on the intracellular calcium concentration and membrane potential as represented by the following equations:
(16)
m∞BK(V)=1.01.0+exp⁡((V−V12)(−15.6)) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} m_{\infty}^{B K}(V)=\frac{1.0}{1.0+\exp \left(\frac{\left(V-V_{\frac{1}{2}}\right)}{(-15.6)}\right)} \tag{16} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(17)
V122=−20+59.2e−90[Ca]+96.7e−470[Ca] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{V_{\frac{1}{2}}}{2}=-20+59.2 e^{-90[C a]}+96.7 e^{-470[C a]} \tag{17} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(18)
τα=−(q−1.0)(f−0.2)0.8+170 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \tau_{\alpha}=-(q-1.0) \frac{(f-0.2)}{0.8}+170 \tag{18} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(19)
q=2.9+6.3e−360[Ca] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle q=2.9+6.3 e^{-360[C a]}$$\end{document}
(20)
s=−25.3+107.5e−120[ca] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} s=-25.3+107.5 e^{-120[c a]} \tag{20} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(21)
f=1.010.0(e−(V+100.0−s)63.6+e(−150.0+(V+100.0−s))63.6) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} f=\frac{1.0}{10.0\left(e^{\frac{-(V+100.0-s)}{63.6}}+e^{\frac{(-150.0+(V+100.0-s))}{63.6}}\right)} \tag{21} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The intracellular concentration of calcium ions is described by the following equation:
(22)
d[Ca]dt=−γILVAzFd+[Ca0]−[Ca]τCa \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{d[C a]}{d t}=-\gamma \frac{I_{L V A}}{z F d}+\frac{\left[C a_{0}\right]-[C a]}{\tau_{C a}} \tag{22} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where Ca0 is the steady state calcium concentration, γ describes calcium buffering, z is the ionic valence of calcium, F is Faraday’s constant and d is the depth in microns at which calcium ions concentrate near the membrane.
